WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.867
A bank's workout unit has closed two hundred and fifty defaulted

00:03.967 --> 00:08.640
small-business loans. On average it recovered forty percent of each exposure,

00:08.740 --> 00:12.732
with a standard deviation of twenty percent. For its provisions it

00:12.832 --> 00:16.513
needs the probability that a loan recovers less than a fifth.

00:18.600 --> 00:21.730
A recovery rate is a proportion. It lives between zero

00:21.830 --> 00:24.482
and one, and it needs a model that knows that.

00:25.566 --> 00:28.599
The beta density lives on the interval from zero to

00:28.699 --> 00:31.854
one, and two parameters shape it. Alpha and beta both

00:31.954 --> 00:34.557
equal to one give the flat, uniform density.

00:34.650 --> 00:39.415
Make them equal and larger, and the curve becomes a symmetric hump that

00:39.515 --> 00:44.417
tightens around one half. Make alpha smaller than beta and the mass moves

00:44.517 --> 00:49.007
toward zero; swap them and it moves toward one. Push both below one

00:49.107 --> 00:52.365
and it turns into a U, piled up at the two edges.

00:52.450 --> 00:55.062
The mean is alpha over alpha plus beta:

00:55.162 --> 00:57.914
alpha pulls toward one, beta toward zero.

00:59.000 --> 01:03.949
Try the normal first, with mean point four and standard deviation point two.

01:04.049 --> 01:08.932
It puts two percent of its probability on recovering less than nothing. Now

01:09.032 --> 01:12.984
try the beta with alpha equal to two and beta equal to three.

01:13.084 --> 01:17.369
Its mean is two fifths, its standard deviation is point two, so it

01:17.469 --> 01:21.887
matches the data, and it never leaves the interval from zero to one.

01:21.983 --> 01:25.956
The probability we want is the area under the beta curve up to

01:26.056 --> 01:31.080
point two. It's a polynomial, so the integral is easy: about eighteen percent.

01:31.180 --> 01:35.153
The normal said sixteen, and got the shape wrong at both ends.

01:36.250 --> 01:39.726
Where does a beta come from? Here's one picture. Drop four

01:39.826 --> 01:43.055
points at random on the interval from zero to one, and

01:43.155 --> 01:46.076
keep the second smallest. Do it again, and again.

01:47.166 --> 01:50.402
Twenty thousand times, the second smallest point piles

01:50.502 --> 01:52.996
up into exactly our beta two, three curve.

01:53.083 --> 01:58.234
And that explains the binomial link. The second smallest is below point two exactly

01:58.334 --> 02:02.472
when at least two of the four points fall below point two. That's a

02:02.572 --> 02:06.585
binomial count with n equal to four and p equal to point two, and

02:06.685 --> 02:10.380
it gives the same eighteen percent, with no integral at all.

02:11.466 --> 02:14.794
So: when the quantity is a proportion, reach for the

02:14.894 --> 02:18.287
beta. Two parameters give it almost any shape on zero

02:18.387 --> 02:21.385
to one, its mean is alpha over alpha plus beta,

02:21.485 --> 02:25.603
and with integer parameters its probabilities are binomial sums.
